er", "Prediction markets price Hobbs near 98%"], "rationale": "Every recent poll, including Republican-sponsored surveys, shows Hobbs ahead by 5 to 13 points, and markets price her near 98% to win. The fundamentals favor the incumbent, and Biggs's narrow fundraising and polarizing profile give little evidence of a late shift.", "watch": "A late polling error or turnout surge among low-propensity conservative voters could tighten the race, as Arizona margins have historically been closer than pre-election polls suggested.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Katie Hobbs (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Democrat Katie Hobbs seeks a second term against Republican Andy Biggs in a state with a slight Republican lean. Polling has been remarkably consistent: every survey since early October shows Hobbs ahead, with recent live-caller polls from both Republican and Democratic sponsors landing in the 5-to-8-point range. Fundraising strongly favors Hobbs, who has raised roughly three times as much as Biggs and holds more cash on hand.\n\nExpert raters mostly call the race Likely Democratic, with a few at Lean or Tilt Democratic, and prediction markets price Hobbs's chances near 98 percent. The main uncertainty is whether polls are overstating her lead, as Arizona races have occasionally been closer than surveys indicated, but the weight of evidence points to a comfortable incumbent win.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9144, "margin": 7.07}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9047, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9201, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9198, "margin": 7.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8839, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8882, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8963, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8934, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8924, "margin": 6.8}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.8871, "margin": 6.5}, {"date":